Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether $\det[I_{i+j}]_{i,j=0}^n = n!! = 1!\cdot 2!\cdots n!$, where $I_k$ counts involutions in $\mathfrak{S}_k$.

**Answer leak check:** No solutions, hints, or answer values are embedded in the problem statement. Proceeding.

Let me first verify small cases, then give a complete proof.

**Small cases.** $I_0=1, I_1=1, I_2=2, I_3=4, I_4=10, I_5=26, I_6=76$ (using $I_n = I_{n-1}+(n-1)I_{n-2}$).

- $n=0$: $\det[I_0]=1=1!\cdot$(empty)$=1$. ✓
- $n=1$: $\det\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix}=1=1!$. ✓
- $n=2$: $\det\begin{pmatrix}1&1&2\\1&2&4\\2&4&10\end{pmatrix}=2=1!\cdot 2!$. ✓
- $n=3$: $\det\begin{pmatrix}1&1&2&4\\1&2&4&10\\2&4&10&26\\4&10&26&76\end{pmatrix}=12=1!\cdot2!\cdot3!$. ✓

The statement appears true. Here is the proof.

